course: dictionary
lesson: 2006
title: Concuss: a knock to the head
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To concuss someone means to hurt their brain with a hard knock to the head. Concuss is the verb and concussion is the noun. A concussion can happen in football, rugby or on a bike, and it can make you dizzy, sick or confused.
explain: The context clues tell you what it feels like: «You don't have to be knocked out to be concussed, and the signs can be odd: asking the score three times, a headache, wobbly legs, feeling sick, blurry eyes or just acting not like yourself.» Words like headache, wobbly and sick show this is about feeling unwell after a hit.
explain: Look at the words «don't have to be knocked out». They tell you that a person can be concussed and still stay awake. Because the signs can be odd, you cannot rely on seeing a bruise or a cut. If you think someone may be concussed, stop playing and tell an adult.
fact: To concuss means to hurt the brain with a hard knock to the head.
fact: You can be concussed without being knocked out.
fact: Signs of concussion can include a headache, wobbly legs, blurry eyes or feeling sick.
fact: A person who is concussed may not seem like themselves, or may keep asking the same question.
fact: If you think you or a friend may be concussed, stop playing and tell an adult straight away.
exercise: «You don't have to be knocked out to be concussed.» What does this tell you? | *You can be concussed even if you stay awake | Concussion only happens in water | A concussion needs you to fall asleep | Only people who are knocked out get hurt
exercise: Which of these is a sign of concussion mentioned in the sentence? | A sunny day | A bright red shirt | A sore toe | *A headache
exercise: What should you do if you think a friend is concussed? | Give them a drink and carry on | Ask them to run it off | *Stop playing and tell an adult | Keep playing so your team does not lose
test: Which word is the noun form of concuss? | Concussing | Concuss | Concusses | *Concussion
test: A player who is concussed has: | *Been hurt by a hard knock to the head | Won a race | Worn a helmet only | Got a new bike
exam: The sentence says the signs «can be odd». What does this suggest about concussion? | *They may not look like a normal injury, such as acting not like yourself | They always mean you need a plaster | They only happen to footballers | They are always loud and easy to spot
